In article <392561C7.955AF7EF@phys.uni-paderborn.de>, Michael Sternberg <sternberg@phys.uni-paderborn.de> writes:

>Kim McCain wrote:
>> A week or so ago, the console started getting /:file system
>> full messages and now the machine won't print although applications seem
>> to work fine. I have already moved home dirs and print queues off the
>> root partition and applications are on another partition. Root actually
>> says it's 108% full.
>
>This is BAD, certainly for users.  Check two places first:
>
>  ls -l /core  /tmp/.
>

To add to the places to look for large files; /dev.  A common user/operator
error is to misspell the name of a tape special file when using the
various tape writing utilities.  They pretty consistently support
writing to ordinary files as well as tapes, so they just treat the
misspelled name as another file to write.

However, this tends to fill up the root completely, and you're not quite
full.  That's why looking in /tmp or for a core file in the root file
system is a good suggestion.

Another thing to consider is the size of the root.  In early versions
of DEC OSF/1, 64 MB was the common root file system size.  Around the
same time as the name changed to Digital UNIX, the suggested root file
system size grew to 128 MB.  If you have an older system that has been
upgrade through a few versions, it may just be that the root is too
small.  Fixing a size problem more closely resembles restoreing the
system after a crash than reinstalling:

Make a backup.
Boot the standalone system off the CDROM.
Repartition the system disk.
Restore from backup.

>Do you have /var on / ?  In that case, check your log files in ~adm/syslog
>for what is causing the pain.  Identify recent big files thus:

I've never actually seen somebody put /var directly under the root.  Bad
idea...


>Read the man pages of find, xargs, du, sort to learn more.

Also look at the hier(5) manual page to see what various
files and directories are for.

>
>Regards,
>-- 
