In article <392D64AB.CC8C1B73@umit.maine.edu>, Steve Cousins <cousins@umit.maine.edu> writes:

>I have a 35/70 Gig DLT drive attached to a DEC Alpha Server 800 running
>OSF/1 4.0.  I am currently using /dev/nrmt2h to back up to it using
>Amanda.
>
>The drive can do compression in Hardware but it doesn't appear that
>compression is actually being used.

Appear how?  If the compression light isn't on when you're writing
that's a good sign that compression isn't being used.  Other methods
are less reliable.

How well data compresses depends on the data.  Sparse files usually
compress very well.  Files that are already compressed in some way
won't compress more and sometimes get larger (though probably not
for this drive).  A data stream that is compressed, such as with
the -C option of vdump can't be compressed again by the drive.

I've read that the TZ89 (the Digital version of the DLT 7000)
had a firmware feature that would trade capacity for better
performance.  If the drive can't sustain a high enough data
rate to media, it has switch from streaming to a start/stop
mode.  By writing extra records that it can pull out later,
it remains the needed data rate to the media, but these records
use up capacity.  This may be a general feature of the DLT 7000,
so the 35/70 will do it as well.

Finally, you probably don't have a entry for this specific model
of DLT 7000 in your /etc/ddr.dbase file.  If compression really
isn't being turned on, this may be the cause.  The format of the
file is reasonably documented in the file, so it should be easy
to adapt the TZ89 entry to the 35/70.

>Do I need to specify a different
>device name to get it to do hardware compression?

The 'h' device should turn on compression, so you've got the
right device.

