What type of swap are you using, immediate or deferred?  If the
latter, that might be contributing to what you're seeing.

By the way, at the risk of putting words in your mouth, you don't
want the system to kill user processes rather than system
processes, you really want the system to manage virtual memory in
a more-graceful fashion such that *no* processes get killed,
correct?  If that is the case, use immediate swap and make sure
you have enough virtual memory (i.e. physical memory + swap
space) to handle the virtual-memory demands of *all* the
processes on the system.

