Axel Preudhomme <preudhomme@mail.dotcom.fr> wrote:
> Hi,
> 
> I'd like to have some insights about disk management :
> Using the Veritas File system,
> 
> 1) Is it possible to have multiple partition on a physical disk
> (for instance "/" and "/usr") ?
> Does a fdisk program exist ?
> 
> 2) What are the requirement of the logging filesystem (special partition ?,
> memory, special daemon, CPU Cycles?)
> 
> 3) Is Veritas Filesystem a standard feature of the OS of a costly option ?
> 
> 4) What are the biggest single physical hard disk that the OS ca manage
> (for instance, it was 8 GB on Sun until Solaris 7) ?
> 
> 5) I understand that a write is faster (because the log is written, the disk
> is written later) but does a read be faster.

Digital/Tru64 Unix does not include the Veritas filesystem.  It does include
a port of the Veritas volume manager (vxvm) called LSM.  If you've used vxvm,
the names have been changed for LSM, but otherwise it's nearly identical to
recent vxvm.

Tru64 Unix has its own advanced filesystem, called (drumroll, please...)
"the Advanced Filesystem" (AdvFS).  It is a journalling filesystem.  It
supports increasing the size of a filesystem, but it does it in a way that's
different from VxFS or IBM's JFS.  It took me a long time to get used to
the different "idiom".  To the best of my knowledge it doesn't support
compressed filesystems (ala JFS) but it does have snapshot capability, which
is really nice in certain circumstances.

Last time I talked to someone from Veritas, their company line was "Tru64 Unix
has AdvFS, so the demand for VxFS isn't enough for us to port it to Tru64".
That may very well have changed in the interim, but you should check to be
sure.

Tru64 Unix is and always has been 64 bit, so file and volume size limits
have long been larger than what folks stuck in the 32 bit world are used to.
As to what disk density Compaq would *support* in a particular box, I'm not
sure.  I've never run into problems with RAID volumes that appear > 8 GB as
far back as 3.2 (many years ago, now), so you should have no problem with
9/18/36 GB drives or "big" raidsets.  The technical limits for filesize,
volume size, # of volumes, etc is part of the SPD (software product
description) for the OS, which you might be able to find on Compaq's site
if you bring a Sherpa or hunting dogs with on your search.  If you can't
find it, email me and and I'll get you the URL.

Tim
