<mooney@dogbert.cc.ndsu.NoDak.edu> wrote:

>In article <39145166$0$11456@bru5-newsr1.be.uu.net>,
>Axel Preudhomme <preudhomme@mail.dotcom.fr> wrote:
>> I'd like to have some insights about disk management :
>> Using the Veritas File system
>> 4) What are the biggest single physical hard disk that the OS ca manage
>> (for instance, it was 8 GB on Sun until Solaris 7) ?
>
>Tru64 Unix is and always has been 64 bit, so file and volume size limits
>have long been larger than what folks stuck in the 32 bit world are used to.
>As to what disk density Compaq would *support* in a particular box, I'm not
>sure.  I've never run into problems with RAID volumes that appear > 8 GB as
>far back as 3.2 (many years ago, now), so you should have no problem with
>9/18/36 GB drives or "big" raidsets.  The technical limits for filesize,
>volume size, # of volumes, etc is part of the SPD (software product
>description) for the OS, which you might be able to find on Compaq's site
>if you bring a Sherpa or hunting dogs with on your search.  If you can't
>find it, email me and and I'll get you the URL.

If you really want Veritas, then this will not be of interest.

The largest single volume that AdvFS can support is 2 terabytes.  This
can be a physical disk (maybe not today, but you have to watch those
disk vendors :-), an LSM volume, a RAID volume, or a SAN volume.

AdvFS will allow you to combine multiple volumes into a single domain
and that domain may be up to 16 terabytes in size.

A single file may be 16 terabytes in size.

The maximum number of files in a fileset is 4 billion.

> 5) I understand that a write is faster (because the log is written, the disk
> is written later) but does a read be faster.

The AdvFS log is used to log metadata changes, not user data.  But
because metadata updates need to be done is a careful order this has
caused other file systems to perform some sync writes to several
locations on the disk with slow seeks in between.  The AdvFS log does
allow the metadata updates to be combined in single transactions and
written to the log with the final update of the metadata taking place
in a controlled but async nature.  Also multiple updates to the same
metadata over time could be combined into a single write.

The user data in a typical UNIX file system has always been written
async, it was just the metadata that needed special sync writing to
maintain file system integrety.

More information about the AdvFS file system can be obtained from the
documentation:

        <http://www.tru64unix.compaq.com/
        faqs/publications/pub_page/doc_list.html>



