Subject: Re: Disaster recovery, how do I do this?
Date: 2000/05/02
Message-ID: <01bfb42b$bac48d20$1d0220c0@mconti2.rime.it>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 618223381
References: <eHyP4.1538$kF4.24341@news1.online.no>
Organization: DSNet ISP (Italy)
X-Server-Date: 2 May 2000 11:45:30 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Must use, in single user mode, ("shutdown now" or "boot -fl s") the vdump
command to save partition on tape:

#vdump -0 /
#vdump -0 /usr

Those commands save dumps on default tape (usually rmt0h)

In case of disaster, you have to boot from Dunix cd, use the disklabel
command to create and make partitions (for AdvFS boot part. use -t advfs)
on new disk after creating the special files in /dev .

Use "mkfdmn" and "mkfset" to create root and usr domain & fileset with the
old names; mount new AdvFS file systems on a "tmp_dir" and use the vrestore
command:

#mount "root_domain_name"#"root_fileset_name" /"tmp_dir"
#cd "tmp_dir"
#vrestore x         (with root dump tape)
#cd /
#umount /"tmp_dir"
#mount "usr_domain_name"#"usr_fileset_name" /"tmp_dir"
#cd "tmp_dir"
#vrestore x         (with usr dump tape)

Shutdown, cross fingers and reboot.

				Marco Conti

